From 9cb3a763f90255a456df286688f267da3352b78f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: az0x <dev@localhost>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 18:38:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: rockchip: add inMusic AZ01/AZ05 device trees

Device trees for inMusic's AZ01 and AZ05 boards (RK3288), covering the four
machines meta-az0x supports -- jp11 (Denon DJ Prime GO), jp13, jc11 and acva
-- plus the rest of both families.

Recovered by decompiling the vendor's shipped DTBs, then factoring the 31
resulting trees against each other rather than carrying 31 near-identical
copies:

  rk3288-az01.dtsi            342 nodes identical on all 27 AZ01 boards
  rk3288-az01-<option>.dtsi   optional blocks, included by the boards with them
  rk3288-az01-<board>-base.dtsi  one board's own nodes
  rk3288-az01-<board>.dts     board = base + its options
  rk3288-az01-<board>-c.dts   RK3288-C speed bin: base + options + small delta

and the same shape for AZ05. That turns 97k lines of decompiler output into
under 20k.

Every one of the 31 trees was verified to rebuild to a DTB semantically
identical to the vendor's original: all 14040 nodes present, no node
differing, none extra, compared phandle-aware so that dtc's renumbering does
not mask a real difference.

rk3288-az01-jp11-fitted.dtsi is the exception to "faithful copy". The shipped
DTB is a template that u-boot edits before boot; on a real Prime GO it changes
exactly four status properties, enabling the ILI2117 touchscreen actually
fitted (the template enables an ILI2116), the bootloader framebuffer handover,
and one USB controller. A kernel booted on the unmodified template has no
working touchscreen, so those four are applied statically. That file also
carries the measured DC-jack threshold for the az01-power driver.

Not upstreamable as-is: no labels (the decompiler discards them, so phandle
references use dtc path syntax), and the root keeps the vendor's 2/2
address/size cells where mainline rk3288.dtsi uses 1/1.

The VOPL IOMMU (iommu@ff940300) is disabled on every board.

u-boot hands the kernel a VOP that is still actively scanning out its splash,
and mainline rockchip-drm has no handover path for a running VOP: the vendor
kernel's "enabled by bootloader" support is not upstream, and the
rockchip,framebuffer-memory reservation is a vendor binding mainline does not
implement. rk_iommu therefore cannot stall the master it is attached to:

    rk_iommu ff940300.iommu: Page fault at 0x7f70c400 of type read
    rk_iommu ff940300.iommu: Enable stall request timed out, status: 0x4b
    rockchip-vop ff940000.vop: failed to get pm runtime: -110
    rockchip-vop ff940000.vop: Runtime PM usage count underflow!
    [CRTC:44:crtc-1] commit wait timed out

The failed enable is followed by an unbalanced atomic_disable, the usage count
underflows, and the device latches into runtime-PM "error" permanently - after
which every atomic commit ends in "flip_done timed out" and the panel stays
black with its backlight on. Without the IOMMU rockchip-drm falls back to
contiguous buffers and never touches the stall path; the cost is scatter-gather
for the framebuffer, which at 800x1280 is affordable.

Confirmed on two boards: a Denon Prime GO (JP11) and an SC6000 (JP13).

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [vendor board support]

These describe inMusic product hardware and are reconstructed from shipped
binaries rather than authored upstream.
